  "content": {
    "title": "Los Angeles school district could close all its schools Tuesday. Why?",
    "text": "April 13, 2026, 2:26 p.m. ETOne of the largest school districts in the nation is at risk of having to close its schools that span Southern California due to a strike being called for on Tuesday, April 14.In mid-March, labor unions representing tens of thousands of workers, including teachers, warned the Los Angeles Unified School District they’d strike unless an agreement was reached by the now impending April date. Union leaders told the USA TODAY Network in March about what they were seeking from the district: an increase in pay was a shared issue, but other key items were related to special education and an increase in staffing.For two unions, gains were recently made. But a strike continues to loom as of the morning of Monday, April 13, for a district that serves hundreds of thousands of students across more than 600 elementary, middle and high schools alone, per district data from 2025.That's because one of three unions has yet to reach an agreement with the district, meaning a strike is still on — one that's brought forth by the union, and that is expected to see the others strike in solidarity.Why could there still be a strike at LAUSD?The district reached a tentative two-year agreement with United Teachers Los Angeles that was announced on Sunday, April 12. More than 37,500 UTLA district teachers and staff serve the district, according to a report in March.Among the wins for UTLA members is an average salary increase of 13.86%, according to the union.Later April 12, the district announced it reached a tentative agreement with the Associated Administrators Los Angeles/Teamsters Local 2010, which represents about 2,900 workers like district principals, according to spokesperson Aimee Baror. The agreement scores its members a salary increase, according to the district.“This amazing victory was only possible due to our solidarity together and with our fellow Union members of UTLA and SEIU Local 99,” the union said. So, what’s the issue?A union that represents 30,000 district workers such as cafeteria workers, custodians and bus drivers is planning to move forward with the strike on April 14 if no agreement is reached in time. That union — Service Employees International United, Local 99 — provided the update on April 12 following the news that UTLA reached a tentative agreement.“Despite UTLA teachers having reached a tentative agreement with the school district, teachers have pledged to stand in solidarity with SEIU Local 99 and join in a sympathy strike,” SEIU Local 99 said in a news release.The union said on social media that UTLA and AALA/Teamsters Local 2010 “are ready to walk out in solidarity with us.”What happens if there is a strike?The district said that all their schools would close if SEIU Local 99 moves forward with a strike. According to district data estimates from last July, nearly 400,000 students, not including those served by independent charter schools, adult education and more, were projected to be enrolled in the 2025-26 academic year.“Labor unions have declared an open-ended strike,” a district spokesperson said in an email on April 13. “In the event a strike moves forward, we will continue to meet with SEIU with the intent to reach an agreement that would allow us to reopen schools as quickly as possible. Families can find resources and the latest status of schools at SchoolUpdates.lausd.org.Paris Barraza is a reporter covering Los Angeles and Southern California for the USA TODAY Network. Reach her at pbarraza@usatodayco.com.",
